{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_cjoky7tg6iutkouvpaxh3a2fsgj6ukc57uxj6vlxbgyth2bky7lq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 31-line translation unit exporting exactly one symbol:\n\n```c\nvoid driver(int x, int y) {\n    div_t result = div(x, y);\n    printf(\"quotient: %d, remainder: %d\\n\", result.quot, result.rem);\n}\n```\n\n## Phase A — surface mapped\n\nThree artifacts created in the crate root, derived mechanically from the C source:\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` on the C `.so` defines exactly one symbol, `driver`. The Rust `.so` exports it. Symbol diff is **empty**; `ldd -r` reports no unresolved symbols.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — grepping the C for every rejection construct returns *nothing*: no `return`, no `assert`, no range/null checks, no error enums. The entire error surface is the hardware trap inside glibc's `div`: **2 rows** (`y == 0`; `INT_MIN / -1`), both → `SIGFPE`.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — no `[features]` in Cargo.toml and no `#ifdef`/`option()` in the C, so the feature power set is a single empty combination. **21 rows** enumerate the input-shape axes the code actually distinguishes (sign × magnitude × divisibility cross-product, boundary values, `%d` output widths, call sequencing/buffering).\n\n## Key finding: the test harness itself was broken, not the Rust\n\nMy first harness redirected fd 1 in-process. Eight tests \"failed\" — but every diff was libtest's own progress output (`\"test row19… \"`, `\"ok\"`) leaking into the capture from parallel threads; the `driver` bytes always matched. I rebuilt the harness to run every comparison in a re-exec'd **worker child** with a private `stdout`. This is both correct under any threading *and* strictly better: it batches thousands of calls per child, and makes `SIGFPE` observable instead of killing the runner.\n\n## Phases B–D — results\n\n**38 tests, all passing** in both dev and release, ~37 000 differential calls per profile. All 21 `CONFIGS.md` and both `ERRORS.md` rows checked off. `verify_all.sh` automates the whole gate.\n\nI verified the tests aren't vacuous by mutation testing (restoring `src/lib.rs` byte-identically after):\n\n| mutation | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| format string capitalisation | format + all comparison rows |\n| swap `idiv` for `wrapping_div`/`wrapping_rem` — *identical values, no traps* | exactly the Phase C rows |\n| Euclidean remainder instead of truncation | the negative-dividend rows |\n| quotient off-by-one only when `x < -1e9 && y > 1e6` | the randomized full-range sweeps |\n| make glibc's `if (numer >= 0 && rem < 0)` fix-up branch live | **nothing — correctly.** On x86-64 `idiv` truncates toward zero, so that branch is provably dead. It's a no-op, not a mutation, which is exactly what CONFIGS row 12 asserts. |\n\nTwo things worth flagging: the translation reproduces the C's *undefined* behaviour deliberately — inline `cdq; idiv` rather than Rust's `/`, so `y == 0` and `INT_MIN / -1` raise `SIGFPE` instead of panicking. I confirmed by disassembly that this survives `-O3` with `panic = \"abort\"` (release still tail-calls `printf` right after `idiv`). And release is verified explicitly, since that's the one config where an `asm!` block could plausibly be optimised away.\n\n**No changes were needed to `src/lib.rs`** — the translation was already correct. I only added `libloading` to `[dev-dependencies]`, the tests, the three artifacts, and `verify_all.sh`. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified (source timestamps unchanged; only the prescribed `build/` directory was created)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":625,"cache_read_input_tokens":137480,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":625,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b755e370-103b-414a-94ee-5de01733975a","uuid":"c786360f-c0fa-4396-8972-220a18c4d1b1","timestamp":"2026-08-18T05:07:23.309Z"}
{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1175683,"num_turns":68,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b755e370-103b-414a-94ee-5de01733975a","total_cost_usd":5.606935249999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":120,"cache_creation_input_tokens":138105,"cache_read_input_tokens":4884958,"output_tokens":92028,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":22304},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":138105},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]":{"inputTokens":120,"outputTokens":92028,"cacheReadInputTokens":4884958,"cacheCreationInputTokens":138105,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":5.606935249999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"bedrock"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"not_first_party","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 31-line translation unit exporting exactly one symbol:\n\n```c\nvoid driver(int x, int y) {\n    div_t result = div(x, y);\n    printf(\"quotient: %d, remainder: %d\\n\", result.quot, result.rem);\n}\n```\n\n## Phase A — surface mapped\n\nThree artifacts created in the crate root, derived mechanically from the C source:\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` on the C `.so` defines exactly one symbol, `driver`. The Rust `.so` exports it. Symbol diff is **empty**; `ldd -r` reports no unresolved symbols.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — grepping the C for every rejection construct returns *nothing*: no `return`, no `assert`, no range/null checks, no error enums. The entire error surface is the hardware trap inside glibc's `div`: **2 rows** (`y == 0`; `INT_MIN / -1`), both → `SIGFPE`.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — no `[features]` in Cargo.toml and no `#ifdef`/`option()` in the C, so the feature power set is a single empty combination. **21 rows** enumerate the input-shape axes the code actually distinguishes (sign × magnitude × divisibility cross-product, boundary values, `%d` output widths, call sequencing/buffering).\n\n## Key finding: the test harness itself was broken, not the Rust\n\nMy first harness redirected fd 1 in-process. Eight tests \"failed\" — but every diff was libtest's own progress output (`\"test row19… \"`, `\"ok\"`) leaking into the capture from parallel threads; the `driver` bytes always matched. I rebuilt the harness to run every comparison in a re-exec'd **worker child** with a private `stdout`. This is both correct under any threading *and* strictly better: it batches thousands of calls per child, and makes `SIGFPE` observable instead of killing the runner.\n\n## Phases B–D — results\n\n**38 tests, all passing** in both dev and release, ~37 000 differential calls per profile. All 21 `CONFIGS.md` and both `ERRORS.md` rows checked off. `verify_all.sh` automates the whole gate.\n\nI verified the tests aren't vacuous by mutation testing (restoring `src/lib.rs` byte-identically after):\n\n| mutation | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| format string capitalisation | format + all comparison rows |\n| swap `idiv` for `wrapping_div`/`wrapping_rem` — *identical values, no traps* | exactly the Phase C rows |\n| Euclidean remainder instead of truncation | the negative-dividend rows |\n| quotient off-by-one only when `x < -1e9 && y > 1e6` | the randomized full-range sweeps |\n| make glibc's `if (numer >= 0 && rem < 0)` fix-up branch live | **nothing — correctly.** On x86-64 `idiv` truncates toward zero, so that branch is provably dead. It's a no-op, not a mutation, which is exactly what CONFIGS row 12 asserts. |\n\nTwo things worth flagging: the translation reproduces the C's *undefined* behaviour deliberately — inline `cdq; idiv` rather than Rust's `/`, so `y == 0` and `INT_MIN / -1` raise `SIGFPE` instead of panicking. I confirmed by disassembly that this survives `-O3` with `panic = \"abort\"` (release still tail-calls `printf` right after `idiv`). And release is verified explicitly, since that's the one config where an `asm!` block could plausibly be optimised away.\n\n**No changes were needed to `src/lib.rs`** — the translation was already correct. I only added `libloading` to `[dev-dependencies]`, the tests, the three artifacts, and `verify_all.sh`. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified (source timestamps unchanged; only the prescribed `build/` directory was created).","ttft_ms":2288,"ttft_stream_ms":1837,"time_to_request_ms":38,"type":"result","duration_ms":1600696,"uuid":"9dfb9f6a-2baa-4475-af55-d48a0936837f"}
